consiSt("ms",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Mississippi","d":"Every appeal from a final trial-court order is filed in the Supreme Court, which keeps some cases and assigns others to the Court of Appeals. It must keep death-penalty, utility-rate, annexation, bond-issue, and election-contest appeals and appeals from rulings that a statute is unconstitutional. It also keeps attorney-discipline, judicial-performance, and federal certified-question matters. It reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary certiorari. It also decides interlocutory appeals by permission (M.R.A.P. 5) and pro hac vice and bar matters."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Appeals of the State of Mississippi","d":"Has only the jurisdiction the Supreme Court gives it by assigning cases (Miss. Code Ann. § 9-4-3, as quoted in M.R.A.P. 16(b)). A party has no right to have its case heard by the Supreme Court instead and may not ask for reassignment. Its decision is final unless the Supreme Court grants certiorari, and a motion for rehearing in the Court of Appeals must come first."},{"l":"TRIAL (EQUITY)","n":"Chancery Courts","d":"Separate equity courts that hear domestic-relations, estate, guardianship, and similar matters, under the Uniform Chancery Court Rules. Many of these matters use the special Rule 4(i) summons (formerly Rule 81(d)), which sets a hearing date and requires no answer. Juries in chancery are 12, with verdicts by 9 or more."},{"l":"TRIAL (LAW)","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts at law, under the Uniform Civil Rules of Circuit and County Court Practice (UCRCCC). Circuit court also sits as an appellate court: appeals from justice and municipal court are tried de novo, and appeals from agencies and other lower authorities are decided on the record. Juries are 12, with verdicts by 9 or more."},{"l":"TRIAL (LIMITED)","n":"County Courts","d":"Civil juries are 6, with verdicts by 5 or more. When a chancery court assigns a case to county court, the county court uses the chancery rules. Interlocutory appeals from county court go to the Supreme Court under M.R.A.P. 5."},{"l":"LOCAL","n":"Justice Courts and Municipal Courts","d":"Courts of limited jurisdiction with their own rules (Uniform Rules of Procedure for Justice Court). A civil judgment of a justice court is appealed to circuit court for trial de novo. The notice of appeal and costs must be filed within 30 days (UCRCCC 5.01, 5.04), and compulsory counterclaims must be added within 30 days (M.R.C.P. 13(k))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute; every 2022–2026 bill died in committee","d":"Mississippi has no anti-SLAPP law. The Legislature’s official Bill Status System lists every measure by session. Its lists for the 2018–2026 regular sessions and the 2025 and 2026 extraordinary sessions show anti-SLAPP bills only in 2022, 2025, and 2026, and each one died in committee:\n• 2022: S.B. 2628 (Public Speech Protection Act).\n• 2025: H.B. 1504 (“Freedom of Speech Protection Act”).\n• 2026: H.B. 1268 and S.B. 2734 (Uniform Public Expression Protection Act), S.B. 2381 (Uniform Public Expression Act), and H.B. 1476 (“Freedom of Speech Protection Act”).\nThe 2026 bills would have created the Act from scratch, which confirms none was already in force. There is no special motion to dismiss, no automatic discovery stay, no fee-shifting, and no interlocutory appeal for speech-based claims. Defendants use ordinary M.R.C.P. 12(b)(6) and 56 motions.\nLimits of this check: it searched bill short titles for 2018–2026, not the full text of the Mississippi Code. The Code is published online only through LexisNexis, a commercial service outside the sources used here.","c":"Miss. Legislature Bill Status System, Reports of All Measures 2018–2026 (searched Nov. 10, 2026); 2026 H.B. 1268, H.B. 1476, S.B. 2381, S.B. 2734","v":true},{"t":"What the failed 2026 bills would have done (not law)","d":"H.B. 1268, the Mississippi Uniform Public Expression Protection Act, would have applied to claims based on: communications in government proceedings; communications on issues under consideration in those proceedings; and the exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights on a matter of public concern. It excluded claims by and against governmental units, provided an appeal, and would have applied to actions filed on or after July 1, 2026. It died in the House Judiciary A Committee on Feb. 3, 2026. Watch for a reintroduced version in the 2027 session.","c":"2026 H.B. 1268 §§ 1–3 (as introduced)","v":true},{"t":"Related rule that does exist: notice before suing a newspaper or broadcaster for libel","d":"Before suing a newspaper, radio station, or television station for libel, the plaintiff must give the defendant written notice at least 10 days in advance, identifying the article or broadcast and the statements claimed to be false. If the defendant acted in good faith on an honest mistake of fact and published a full and fair correction and retraction within 10 days after the notice, the plaintiff recovers only actual damages. The defendant must plead and prove these facts as affirmative defenses. The rule does not apply to editorials, opinion columns, or statements about a candidate made within 10 days of an election. This is a damages limit, not an early-dismissal tool.\nThe text comes from 2026 H.B. 1476, which brought § 95-1-5 forward unchanged; it was not checked against the Code itself.","c":"Miss. Code Ann. § 95-1-5 (text as brought forward in 2026 H.B. 1476 § 15)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is governed by appellate Rule 46(b) for every court and agency","d":"A foreign attorney (licensed and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction but not in Mississippi) may appear only as permitted by M.R.A.P. 46(b). This applies in every Mississippi court and before state and local agencies, not just on appeal. “Appearance” is broad. It covers having your name on any filed or served paper, appearing in person, and taking part in a deposition or other proceeding where testimony is given. Presenting uncontested matters to an agency is not an appearance. The appearance lasts until final determination or an order allowing withdrawal.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(1)–(2)","v":true},{"t":"What to file, and where","d":"File with the trial court or agency and with the Clerk of the Supreme Court:\n(1) a verified application; and\n(2) a statement from the Clerk of the Supreme Court listing every earlier pro hac vice request you made in Mississippi, with its date and outcome.\nServe both on all parties with a certificate of service. The application must list:\n• every jurisdiction where you are admitted;\n• your good standing, and any discipline in the past 5 years;\n• every Mississippi pro hac vice matter in the past 12 months, plus any pending matters or requests;\n• that you keep no office in Mississippi (unless your firm has a Mississippi office staffed full time by Mississippi lawyers);\n• your consent to Mississippi courts and disciplinary tribunals.\nThe associated local attorney must also sign it. No hearing is required unless a party moves or the court wants one.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(5)–(6)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: $300 to the Mississippi Bar plus the Supreme Court docket fee","d":"When you file the application, pay $300 to The Mississippi Bar, which funds legal services for the indigent. Certify that payment to the court or agency and to the Supreme Court Clerk. Also pay the Supreme Court Clerk the customary miscellaneous docket fee under Miss. Code Ann. § 25-7-3; the amount is set by statute and is not stated in the rule. The court or agency may waive fees for indigent clients for good cause.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(5)","v":true},{"t":"Do not appear until the order is lodged with the Supreme Court Clerk","d":"You may not appear as counsel pro hac vice until you certify to the court or agency that you have given the Supreme Court Clerk a copy of the order authorizing the appearance. Clerks must refuse papers from a foreign attorney who has not complied, and noncompliant papers are struck. A violation is the unlawful practice of law. One exception: requesting a subpoena under M.R.C.P. 45(a)(3) is not subject to the rule.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(7), (b)(11)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must sign everything, attend trial, and share liability","d":"You must associate a Mississippi lawyer in the case, and that lawyer’s name must appear on every notice, order, pleading, and paper. The local lawyer must personally attend every trial. Unless the court or agency excuses it, the local lawyer must also attend every pretrial conference, open-court hearing, deposition, and other proceeding in Mississippi where testimony is taken. Local counsel is jointly and severally responsible with you to the client, opposing parties, and the tribunal. If local counsel is suspended, disbarred, or incapacitated, you must associate new local counsel and amend the application before proceeding.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(4), (b)(9)(ii)","v":true},{"t":"Limit: more than 5 unrelated matters in 12 months is “general practice”","d":"Appearing in more than 5 separate, unrelated causes or matters in Mississippi courts or agencies in the 12 months before the appearance counts as the general practice of law, which only members of the Mississippi Bar may do. You also cannot appear pro hac vice if you keep an office in Mississippi, unless your firm’s Mississippi office is staffed full time by Mississippi-licensed lawyers. Full-time law teachers at Mississippi law schools are exempt from these limits but must still associate local counsel. A finding that you or your firm advertised in violation of Mississippi Rule 7.2 bars pro hac vice admission.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(1)(iii), (b)(3), (b)(8)","v":true},{"t":"Permission ends automatically on suspension or disbarment anywhere","d":"Pro hac vice permission ends if you are suspended or disbarred in any jurisdiction where you are admitted. You must promptly report discipline from any other jurisdiction to the Mississippi court or agency. The judge may revoke permission and cite you for contempt for misconduct during the appearance, and must refer the matter to the Bar’s disciplinary counsel. Lawyers for the United States may appear without local counsel.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(9)(i), (b)(10)–(11)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and in-house work without pro hac vice (Rule 5.5)","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not suspended or disbarred may provide temporary services in Mississippi if one of these applies:\n• the work is done with a Mississippi lawyer who actively participates;\n• it relates to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear;\n• it relates to an arbitration or mediation (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or\n• it arises from the lawyer’s home-state practice.\nUnlike the ABA Model Rule, the last option excludes drafting documents to be recorded that affect interests in real property.\nIn-house lawyers and lawyers authorized by federal or state law may practice under Rule 5.5(d). They must register with the Mississippi Bar every year and pay a fee equal to an active member’s dues.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 5.5(c)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without exam: comity after 5 years of practice, only with reciprocal states","d":"A lawyer from another state may be admitted without the bar exam after at least 5 years of active practice, but only if that state grants similar privileges to Mississippi lawyers and its admission requirements are equivalent. The Board of Bar Admissions decides whether a state is reciprocal. The fee is $1,500 plus NCBE investigation charges, and it is not refundable. If your home state imposes stricter conditions or higher fees on Mississippi lawyers, those conditions and fees apply to you. You are ineligible if you failed the Mississippi bar exam within the past 5 years. The comment says comity is not a route to temporary practice; pro hac vice is.","c":"Rules Governing Admission to the Mississippi Bar, Rule VI §§ 1, 4, 5, 7 & cmt. (as amended through May 30, 2019)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation: allowed by Rule 1.2(c), but withdrawal still needs leave","d":"A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The comment encourages limited services such as attending a single deposition or hearing, or handling a specific issue. The collected court rules contain no special limited-appearance or automatic-withdrawal procedure. In circuit and county court, an attorney who has appeared may withdraw only with the court’s permission, after notice to the client and all counsel. On appeal, a withdrawal motion must include substitute counsel’s appearance or the client’s signed agreement to proceed pro se, or explain why neither can be had.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.2(c) & cmt.; UCRCCC 1.13; M.R.A.P. 46(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: MEC in the trial courts, mandatory electronic filing in the appellate courts","d":"Trial courts that have adopted the Mississippi Electronic Courts system (MEC) require documents to be filed electronically or scanned by the clerk. Attorneys admitted pro hac vice may register as MEC users; pro se non-lawyers may not. Each attorney registers individually and pays a registration fee, and registration waives conventional service.\nIn the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals, briefs, motions, responses, and compliance documents must be filed electronically by a registered attorney (or scanned by the Clerk) since Jan. 1, 2014. Pro hac vice attorneys may register for that system too.","c":"MEC Administrative Procedures §§ 1(E), 2(A)–(B) (eff. Oct. 25, 2018); Appellate E-Filing Administrative Procedures §§ 1(D), 2(A); M.R.A.P. 25(a)(1); UCRCCC 1.16","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Mississippi Rules of Professional Conduct (effective July 1, 1987, as amended)","d":"Mississippi follows the ABA Model Rules structure but has kept older wording in many places and has its own provisions. Read the Mississippi text rather than assuming Model Rule language. The examples below come straight from the rule text.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct (courts.ms.gov)","v":true},{"t":"Living-expense advances to litigation clients: allowed, capped at $1,500, reported within 7 days","d":"Unlike Model Rule 1.8(e), Mississippi lets a lawyer advance a client’s reasonable and necessary medical and minimal living expenses tied to the litigation, with strict conditions:\n• only in dire and necessitous circumstances, such as preventing foreclosure;\n• only after 60 days have passed since the client signed the fee contract;\n• no promises of future payments, and no mention of such payments in public communications.\nTotal advances to one party from all lawyers in the litigation are capped at $1,500 unless the Bar’s Standing Committee on Ethics approves more on an ex parte application. Each payment must be reported to that committee within 7 days. Court costs and litigation expenses, including medical expenses needed to prepare the case, may also be advanced, with repayment contingent on the outcome.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.8(e)","v":true},{"t":"Advertisements must be filed with the Bar’s General Counsel before first use","d":"A copy or recording of any advertisement must be submitted to the Office of the General Counsel of the Mississippi Bar before it is first disseminated. The fee is $25 per submission, or $150 if the submission is late. Exemptions include tombstone-type ads limited to Rule 7.2/7.4 information, telephone directory ads, business cards, letterhead, office signs, websites viewed through a search the user starts, and mail to existing or former clients or other lawyers. The Model Rules have no pre-filing requirement. An adverse finding under Rule 7.2 also bars pro hac vice admission.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 7.5(a)–(c); M.R.A.P. 46(b)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: narrower list of exceptions, plus Lawyers and Judges Assistance rules","d":"Rule 1.6(b) allows disclosure to prevent death or bodily harm; to prevent, mitigate, or rectify a client crime or fraud that used the lawyer’s services; to get ethics advice; for lawyer-client controversies; and to comply with law or a court order. The Mississippi text has no Model Rule 1.6(b)(7) exception for detecting conflicts when changing firms, and no Model Rule 1.6(c) duty to make reasonable efforts against unauthorized disclosure. Mississippi adds confidentiality rules for interventions by the Lawyers and Judges Assistance Committee, along with a duty to report compliance information to that committee.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Older Model Rule provisions still in force: intermediary rule and no prospective-client rule","d":"Mississippi keeps Rule 2.2 (lawyer as intermediary between clients), which the ABA deleted in 2002. A lawyer may act as an impartial intermediary for two or more clients working toward a common objective, if the conditions in 2.2(b) are met. The table of rules has no Rule 1.18 (duties to prospective clients). Rule 1.10 is still titled “Imputed Disqualification: General Rule.” When a conflict question turns on these rules, use the Mississippi text and Mississippi ethics opinions, not the current Model Rules.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.10, 2.2 (table of rules)","v":true},{"t":"Fee rules: written contingent-fee agreements; fee splits without written client consent","d":"A contingent-fee agreement must be in writing and state:\n• the percentage due on settlement, trial, or appeal;\n• which expenses are deducted; and\n• whether expenses are deducted before or after the fee is calculated.\nAt the end, the lawyer must give the client a written closing statement. Contingent fees are barred in divorce, alimony, and support matters and for criminal defense.\nA fee split between firms must be proportional to the services performed, or each lawyer must assume joint responsibility by written agreement with the client. The client need only be “advised of and not object to” the participation of all the lawyers. Unlike Model Rule 1.5(e), there is no requirement that the client agree to each lawyer’s share in a writing.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Choice of law and discipline of non-Mississippi lawyers","d":"A lawyer not admitted in Mississippi is subject to Mississippi discipline for providing or offering legal services there. For conduct in a matter before a tribunal, the rules of the tribunal’s jurisdiction apply. Pro hac vice counsel must study and comply with the Mississippi rules and are subject to the courts, the Bar’s disciplinary tribunals, and the Board of Bar Admissions.","c":"Miss. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.5; M.R.A.P. 46(b)(3)","v":true}],"style":"State of Mississippi","home":"https://courts.ms.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Post-trial motions: 10 days from entry, and no extensions","d":"Motions for JNOV, amended findings, a new trial, or to alter or amend the judgment are due 10 days after entry of judgment, not 28 as in federal court. The trial court cannot extend them. A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if it is filed within 10 days after entry of judgment.","c":"M.R.C.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); M.R.A.P. 4(d)"},{"t":"No appellate extension of the civil appeal deadline","d":"The notice of appeal is due 30 days after entry of the judgment and is filed with the trial-court clerk. A late notice means mandatory dismissal. Neither appellate court can extend the time to appeal, or to petition for interlocutory appeal, in a civil case. Only the trial court can extend it under Rule 4(g): good cause if you move before the time runs out, excusable neglect if you move within the next 30 days, and the extension can last at most 30 days or 10 days after the order, whichever is later.","c":"M.R.A.P. 2(a)(1), 2(c), 4(a), 4(g), 26(b)"},{"t":"Seven days after the notice of appeal: designate the record and deposit costs","d":"Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal, you must:\n• file and serve a designation of the record;\n• deposit the estimated record cost with the trial-court clerk; and\n• file and serve a certificate of compliance.\nIf the court reporter gives no estimate, the deposit is $300 per day of proceedings. Missing these steps does not void the appeal but can lead to sanctions or dismissal. After a deficiency notice, you have 14 days to cure.","c":"M.R.A.P. 2(a)(2), 10(b)(1), 11(b)(1)"},{"t":"Answer extensions by agreement: once only, 10 days, filed stipulation","d":"The answer is due 30 days after service. The parties may extend it only once, by no more than 10 days, and only by a written stipulation of counsel filed in the record. Open-ended courtesy extensions are not authorized by the rule.","c":"M.R.C.P. 12(a)"},{"t":"Circuit and county court: discovery closes 90 days after the answer","d":"Unless the court grants leave on a written motion showing good cause, discovery must be completed within 90 days after the defendant serves its answer. Absent special circumstances, an expert not designated at least 60 days before trial may not testify. Discovery responses must restate each request before the answer. A motion to compel must include a good-faith conference certificate and quote each disputed request verbatim.","c":"UCRCCC 4.03"},{"t":"Motion practice: memoranda go to the judge, not the clerk, and unheard motions are abandoned","d":"In circuit court, file the motion with the clerk and mail a copy to the judge. Memoranda on dismissal and summary-judgment motions are mailed to the judge and not filed with the clerk. The response is due within 10 days and the rebuttal within 5. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages. You must attach copies of any authority that is not a Mississippi or federal statute or rule, a U.S. Supreme Court case, or a case in the Southern or Federal Reporter. The movant must bring its motion to hearing: a pretrial motion not pursued before trial is deemed abandoned, and dispositive motions are abandoned unless heard at least 10 days before trial.","c":"UCRCCC 2.04, 4.02"},{"t":"Requests for admission are conclusively admitted after 30 days","d":"A request for admission is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days. A defendant need not answer before 45 days after service of the summons. Deemed admissions are conclusively established unless the court allows withdrawal.","c":"M.R.C.P. 36"},{"t":"Serve within 120 days, and know Mississippi’s mail and residence-service rules","d":"An unserved defendant is dismissed without prejudice after 120 days unless you show good cause. Service by first-class mail works only if the defendant returns the acknowledgment form. Certified mail (return receipt requested) is an added option only for persons outside Mississippi. Residence service is allowed only if personal service cannot be made with reasonable diligence. It requires leaving the papers with the spouse or a family member above 16 and then mailing a copy, and service is complete on the 10th day after mailing.","c":"M.R.C.P. 4(c)(3), 4(c)(5), 4(d)(1)(B), 4(h)"},{"t":"Recusal: 30-day deadline from notice of the assigned judge","d":"A recusal motion must be supported by an affidavit and filed within 30 days after the parties are told which judge is assigned. The only exception is facts that could not reasonably have been known then. Review of a denial is by petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days.","c":"UCRCCC 1.15; UCCR 1.11; M.R.A.P. 48B"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: local counsel must attend trials, hearings, and depositions","d":"Mississippi local counsel cannot be a name on the pleadings only. Local counsel must personally attend every trial and, unless excused, every hearing, pretrial conference, and deposition in the state. Local counsel is also jointly and severally responsible. Papers you file before the pro hac vice order is lodged with the Supreme Court Clerk are struck.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(b)(4), (b)(7), (b)(11)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal: 21 days, straight to the Supreme Court, no trial-court certification","d":"Petition the Supreme Court within 21 days after the order is entered, and serve the trial judge and all parties. Unlike 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), no trial-court certification is needed. The petition is limited to 15 pages including the brief, and no reply is allowed. The comment condemns seeking belated reconsideration to restart the 21 days. A petition does not stay trial-court proceedings unless ordered.","c":"M.R.A.P. 5(a)–(c), (f) & cmt."},{"t":"Certiorari from the Court of Appeals requires a rehearing motion first","d":"To reach the Supreme Court after a Court of Appeals decision, first move for rehearing in the Court of Appeals within 14 days. Then petition for certiorari within 14 days after the ruling on rehearing. The petition is limited to 10 pages, and the opinion and rehearing motion must be attached. Silence for 90 days after the response deadline is a denial.","c":"M.R.A.P. 17(b), (e), 40(a)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of a trial judge; sworn recusal motion within 30 days","d":"The collected Mississippi rules provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. Recusal is for cause, when a reasonable person who knows all the circumstances might question the judge’s impartiality, or on other grounds in the Code of Judicial Conduct or by law.\n• Form: the motion must come with an affidavit of the party or its attorney stating the facts and swearing good faith.\n• Deadline: file it with the judge being challenged within 30 days after the parties are told which judge is assigned. If the facts could not reasonably have been known then, file within 30 days after they could reasonably be discovered.\n• Ruling: the judge must rule within 30 days, and any hearing must be on the record in open court.\nIn chancery the same rule applies, but the motion is served on the chancellor.","c":"M.R.C.P. 16A; UCRCCC 1.15; UCCR 1.11; Code of Judicial Conduct Canon 3E","v":true},{"t":"Review of a denied recusal motion: petition the Supreme Court within 14 days","d":"If a circuit, chancery, or county judge denies recusal, or has not ruled within 30 days of the motion, the movant may petition the Supreme Court within 14 days after the ruling, or within 14 days after the 30-day period ends. Attach the recusal order and the hearing transcript. The Supreme Court orders recusal only for an abuse of discretion, and the procedure follows M.R.A.P. 21 (extraordinary writs).","c":"M.R.A.P. 48B","v":true},{"t":"Appellate justices and judges: motion due 30 days after assignment notice","d":"A motion to recuse a Supreme Court justice or Court of Appeals judge needs the same kind of good-faith affidavit. File it within 30 days after the clerk gives notice of which court the case is assigned to, or within 30 days after the facts could reasonably be discovered. The challenged justice or judge decides first. Reconsideration by the whole court must be sought within 14 days of the denial. Court of Appeals recusal rulings reach the Supreme Court only after reconsideration is denied, by motion within 14 days.","c":"M.R.A.P. 48C","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"State constitutional speech protections: not established here","d":"The text of the Mississippi Constitution’s free-speech provisions, and any case law reading them more broadly than the First Amendment, was not collected. Do not argue an independent state standard without checking the constitution and case law.","c":"Miss. Const. (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Separate chancery courts, and a special summons for family and estate matters","d":"Mississippi keeps separate courts of equity (chancery) and law (circuit). Since July 1, 2026, M.R.C.P. 4(i), formerly Rule 81(d), governs listed family, estate, and similar matters, such as custody, support, paternity, heirship, will contests, modification, and contempt. These use a Form 1D/1DD summons returnable on a set date, 7 or 30 days after service depending on the matter. No answer is required, and there is no default. Older cases and the Advisory Committee Note still cite “Rule 81(d)” for these procedures.","c":"M.R.C.P. 4(i), 81(d) (eff. July 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Non-unanimous civil juries","d":"Circuit and chancery juries have 12 jurors, and 9 or more decide. County court juries have 6, and 5 or more decide. No jury demand is required: the right is waived only by a specific written stipulation filed with the court, and the court may still order a jury.","c":"M.R.C.P. 38, 48","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Mississippi Rules of Appellate Procedure, official consolidated text posted by the Supreme Court of Mississippi (courts.ms.gov, “Rules of Appellate Procedure Current”), reflecting the amendment to Rule 2(a)(2) effective June 19, 2026 (order of May 20, 2026, No. 89-R-99027-SCT). Also: Appellate E-Filing Administrative Procedures; M.R.C.P. 6, 50, 52, 59, 60 (2026-07-01 text); Uniform Civil Rules of Circuit and County Court Practice 4.05, 5.01–5.08. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Mississippi: file everything in the Supreme Court, then live with the assignment","d":"Every appeal from a final trial-court judgment starts with a notice of appeal filed with the trial-court clerk within 30 days. The appeal is docketed in the Supreme Court, which either keeps the case or assigns it to the Court of Appeals. A party has no right to a Supreme Court hearing, except in the categories that by statute the Supreme Court must keep, and cannot ask for reassignment. A Court of Appeals decision is final unless the Supreme Court grants discretionary certiorari, and a rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals is a prerequisite.\nTwo features surprise lawyers used to federal practice:\n• Post-trial motions are due 10 days after judgment, with no extensions.\n• The appellate courts can never extend the time to appeal in a civil case.\nWithin 7 days after the notice, the appellant must designate the record and deposit estimated costs. Briefs are limited by pages (50 principal, 25 reply), not words. Filing in both appellate courts is electronic."},"groups":[["Which court, and what is appealable",[{"t":"One procedure, one filing court","d":"Appeals of right in civil and criminal cases are perfected only by timely filing a notice of appeal with the trial-court clerk. Every other step is enforced by sanctions, not jurisdiction. All appeals from final trial-court orders are filed in the Supreme Court, which assigns cases to the Court of Appeals.","c":"M.R.A.P. 3(a), 16(a)"},{"t":"Cases the Supreme Court keeps","d":"The Supreme Court may not assign appeals involving the death penalty, utility rates, annexations, bond issues, election contests, or a trial-court ruling that a statute is unconstitutional. It also keeps attorney discipline, judicial performance, and federal certified questions. It ordinarily keeps major questions of first impression, urgent issues of broad public importance, substantial constitutional challenges, and conflicts between decisions. The appellant’s brief must include a Statement of Assignment explaining why the Supreme Court must or should keep the case.","c":"M.R.A.P. 16(b), (d), 28(a)(4)"},{"t":"No reassignment requests","d":"Once a case is assigned to the Court of Appeals, neither a party nor that court may ask for reassignment. Only the Supreme Court may move it on its own motion. A party has no right to be heard by the Supreme Court.","c":"M.R.A.P. 16(d)–(e)"},{"t":"Partial judgments","d":"Interlocutory review of a judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is available through an M.R.C.P. 54(b) final-judgment certification. This is separate from Rule 5 permissive appeals.","c":"M.R.A.P. 5 cmt.; M.R.C.P. 54(b)"},{"t":"Appeals to circuit court from justice court, municipal court, and agencies","d":"Direct appeals from justice and municipal court are tried de novo in circuit court. All other appeals to circuit court, including from agencies and local governing bodies, are on the record. In both, the notice of appeal and the costs must be filed and paid with the circuit clerk within 30 days of entry of the order or judgment, with a certificate of service. In an on-the-record appeal, the record is due within 30 days after the notice. Agency decisions are reviewed only for substantial evidence, arbitrariness, lack of power, or violation of statutory or constitutional rights. Perfecting the appeal is not a supersedeas; posting a bond of 125% of a money judgment stays only the money part.","c":"UCRCCC 5.01, 5.03–5.05, 5.08"}]],["Notice of appeal: time, tolling, and cross-appeals",[{"t":"30 days from entry, filed with the trial-court clerk","d":"File the notice with the trial-court clerk within 30 days after the judgment or order is entered. A notice mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court is sent back down and treated as filed on the date the Supreme Court received it. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. The notice must name the appealing parties and the parties appealed against and designate the judgment. Informality of form does not defeat the appeal. The appellant pays the statutory docket fee to the trial-court clerk when filing.","c":"M.R.A.P. 3(c), (e), 4(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and other parties: 14 days after the first notice","d":"Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice is filed, or within the normal 30 days if that is later. A cross-appeal requires its own notice of appeal.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(c) & cmt."},{"t":"Tolling motions, all due 10 days after entry","d":"A timely motion under M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), or 59, or a Rule 60 motion filed within 10 days after entry, restarts the appeal time for all parties. The new time runs from the order disposing of the last such motion. Each motion is due 10 days after entry of judgment, and the trial court cannot extend that time. A late “motion to reconsider” therefore does not toll the time.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(d); M.R.C.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)"},{"t":"Premature notices ripen, and no amended notice is needed","d":"A notice filed before the tolling motions are decided takes effect once the last motion is decided, and it covers the order deciding the motion too. Unlike the federal rule, no amended notice is required. The comment still advises filing after the motions are decided.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(d) & cmt."},{"t":"Extension only by the trial court; appellate courts cannot extend","d":"The trial court may extend the time on a motion filed no later than 30 days after the appeal time expires. A motion filed before the time expires needs good cause and may be ex parte; one filed after needs notice and excusable neglect. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 10 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. The appellate courts may not extend the time to appeal, or to petition for permission to appeal, in a civil case. A late notice requires dismissal.","c":"M.R.A.P. 2(a)(1), 2(c), 4(g), 26(b)"},{"t":"Reopening when notice of the judgment was not received","d":"If a party did not receive notice of entry from the clerk or any party within 21 days, and no party would be prejudiced, the trial court may reopen the time for 14 days. The motion must be filed within 180 days of entry or within 7 days of receiving notice, whichever is earlier. Winning parties are encouraged to serve their own notice of entry to cut off this period.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(h) & cmt."},{"t":"Parties under disability, and taxpayer appeals","d":"For minors and persons of unsound mind, the appeal periods do not start until the disability is removed. If such a person was the plaintiff, or was a defendant with a guardian ad litem, the appeal must be taken within 2 years of entry. If a county or municipal board or officer sued in an official capacity does not appeal within 20 days, or within 7 days after another party’s notice, any taxpayer may prosecute the appeal at the taxpayer’s own expense.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(f), 4(i)"}]],["Record, costs, and stays",[{"t":"Seven-day steps: designation and cost deposit","d":"Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal:\n• Designate the record. File it with the trial-court clerk and serve it on the court reporter and the appellee. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported by or contrary to the evidence, include a transcript of all the evidence relevant to it.\n• Deposit the estimated cost of the record with the trial-court clerk. File a certificate of compliance and serve it on all parties, the reporter, and the Supreme Court Clerk. If the reporter gives no estimate, compute it at $300 per day of proceedings.\nThe appellee may designate additional parts within 14 days after service.","c":"M.R.A.P. 10(b)(1)–(2), (b)(4), 11(b)(1)"},{"t":"Transcript timing and record checks","d":"The reporter has 60 days from service of the designation to file the transcript. The trial court may grant one 30-day extension; any later extension comes from the Supreme Court Clerk. Counsel then have 14 days to examine the record and propose corrections. The trial-court clerk assembles the clerk’s papers within 30 days.","c":"M.R.A.P. 10(b)(5), 11(c)–(d)"},{"t":"Supersedeas: 125% bond approved by the clerk","d":"A bond of 125% of a money judgment, approved by the trial-court clerk, stays execution. Other stays, and contested or reduced bonds, go first to the trial court, which may set a lower bond for good cause. For punitive damages, the bond is the lower of 125% of the punitive award or 10% of the defendant’s net worth, capped at $100,000,000 absent unusual circumstances. The net-worth option requires showing that the full bond would threaten insolvency. A motion in the appellate court must show that seeking relief in the trial court was impracticable or was denied.","c":"M.R.A.P. 8(a)–(c)"}]],["Briefs: schedule, contents, and limits",[{"t":"Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14 days","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is filed, the appellee’s brief 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief, and the reply 14 days after that. In cross-appeals, the combined responsive brief is due 30 days after the appellee/cross-appellant’s brief, and the cross-appellant’s reply 14 days after that. The deadlines run even if the clerk’s notice that the record was filed never arrives.","c":"M.R.A.P. 31(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Page limits, not word counts","d":"Principal briefs are limited to 50 pages and reply briefs to 25. Pages for the oral-argument statement, certificates, tables, and statutory addendum do not count. Text must be at least 12-point and footnotes at least 11-point, double-spaced, with a 1½-inch left margin. The clerk may raise the limit to 75 pages on an unopposed motion (125 in death-penalty appeals).","c":"M.R.A.P. 27(b)(5), 28(h), 32(a)"},{"t":"Required contents unique to Mississippi","d":"The appellant’s brief starts with a Certificate of Interested Persons, which is used for recusal review (government parties are exempt). It must also include a Statement of Assignment and numbered issues; issues not distinctly identified are not argued, except plain error. Each brief cover must state whether oral argument is requested.","c":"M.R.A.P. 28(a)(1)–(4), 32 cmt."},{"t":"Record excerpts filed with the appellant’s brief","d":"With the brief, the appellant must file separately indexed record excerpts containing the trial-court docket, the judgment or order appealed from, the other rulings under review, and the trial court’s findings or opinion. The appellee may add its own excerpts.","c":"M.R.A.P. 30(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"An amicus brief requires leave of court, except one presented by the State through the Attorney General or by a non-party guardian ad litem. The motion is due within 7 days after the initial brief of the party the amicus supports, and the proposed brief is limited to 15 pages. A party may oppose within 7 days.","c":"M.R.A.P. 29"},{"t":"Failure to file and deficiency notices","d":"An appeal may be dismissed for failure to prosecute or for substantial noncompliance, after the clerk sends a deficiency notice and 14 days pass without a cure. The 2026 amendment deleted the sentence barring motions for more time to file briefs after a deficiency notice. An appellee who files no brief may not argue without permission.","c":"M.R.A.P. 2(a)(2) (as amended eff. June 19, 2026), 31(d)"}]],["Motions, rehearing, and certiorari",[{"t":"Motions","d":"A response is due 7 days after service. The clerk may rule on unopposed procedural motions, including extensions totaling up to 60 days, extra brief pages, withdrawal or substitution of counsel, voluntary dismissal, and pro hac vice appearances.","c":"M.R.A.P. 27(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, 25 pages, no oral argument","d":"A motion for rehearing is due 14 days after the merits decision of either court. It must state with particularity what the court overlooked or misapprehended, is limited to 25 pages, and is decided without oral argument. Any response is due within 7 days. After a denial, no further rehearing motion is allowed; after a grant, a second motion may be filed within 14 days of the judgment on rehearing.","c":"M.R.A.P. 40(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Certiorari to the Supreme Court","d":"A rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals is required first. The petition is due within 14 days after the Court of Appeals rules on rehearing. It is limited to 10 pages, may not incorporate other documents by reference, and must attach the opinion, the judgment, and the rehearing motion. A response (optional, 10 pages) is due within 7 days. No briefs or argument are allowed unless requested. Four justices must vote to grant. If the Court does not act within 90 days after the response deadline, the petition is deemed denied. If certiorari is granted, supplemental briefs (10 pages) are due within 10 days.","c":"M.R.A.P. 17(a)–(h)"},{"t":"Mandate","d":"The Supreme Court’s mandate issues 21 days after judgment, or 7 days after rehearing is denied. The Court of Appeals’ mandate issues 21 days after the latest of judgment, decision on rehearing, or denial or dismissal of certiorari. A stay of mandate pending a U.S. Supreme Court petition is limited to 90 days unless extended.","c":"M.R.A.P. 41(a)–(c)"}]],["Interlocutory review and writs",[{"t":"Permissive interlocutory appeal: 21-day petition to the Supreme Court","d":"The petition must show a substantial basis for difference of opinion on a question of law. It must also show that review may materially advance the end of the litigation and avoid exceptional expense, protect a party from substantial and irreparable injury, or resolve an issue of general importance. File it with the Supreme Court Clerk within 21 days after the order is entered, and serve the trial judge and all parties. No trial-court certification is required.\nThe trial judge may file a statement and adverse parties may answer within 14 days. No reply is allowed, and the petition and answer are limited to 15 pages each. If permission is granted, pay the docket fee within 14 days; no notice of appeal is needed. The petition does not stay the trial court unless ordered.","c":"M.R.A.P. 5(a)–(d), (f) & cmt.; UCRCCC 4.05"},{"t":"Recusal review and extraordinary writs","d":"Denial of a trial-judge recusal motion, or failure to rule within 30 days, may be reviewed by petition to the Supreme Court within 14 days, under the M.R.A.P. 21 writ procedure. The standard is abuse of discretion.","c":"M.R.A.P. 21, 48B"}]],["Filing, service, time computation, and e-filing",[{"t":"Electronic filing is mandatory for attorneys","d":"Since Jan. 1, 2014, briefs, motions, responses, and compliance documents in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals must be filed electronically by a registered attorney, or are scanned by the Clerk. Pro hac vice attorneys may register. Conventionally filed briefs and record excerpts are timely when mailed, if they include a certificate of mailing; other papers are filed when received.","c":"M.R.A.P. 25(a)(1)–(2); Appellate E-Filing Administrative Procedures §§ 1(D), 2(A)"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the trigger day. A last day that falls on a weekend, a legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed rolls to the next business day. For periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. Add 3 days after service by mail.","c":"M.R.A.P. 26(a), (c)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice on appeal","d":"Lawyers not admitted in Mississippi may file or argue only after pro hac vice admission under Rule 46(b), with local counsel, the $300 Bar payment, and filing with the Supreme Court Clerk. Withdrawal requires court approval.","c":"M.R.A.P. 46(a)–(c)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nFiled with the trial-court clerk. Mandatory dismissal if late; the appellate courts cannot extend it in civil cases (M.R.A.P. 2(a)(1), 2(c)).","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Filed with the trial-court clerk. Mandatory dismissal if late; the appellate courts cannot extend it in civil cases (M.R.A.P. 2(a)(1), 2(c))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the 30-day period, if later).","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Tolling post-trial motions (M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59; Rule 60 to toll)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (M.R.C.P. 6(b)). Appeal time then runs from the order disposing of the last motion.","c":"M.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); M.R.A.P. 4(d)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (M.R.C.P. 6(b)). Appeal time then runs from the order disposing of the last motion."}},{"t":"Motion in trial court to extend appeal time","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal time.\nExcusable neglect after expiration. Extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(g)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal time)","note":"Excusable neglect after expiration. Extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion to reopen appeal time (no notice of entry received)","d":"180 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nIf granted, the time is reopened for 14 days from the order.","c":"M.R.A.P. 4(h)","due":{"n":180,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"If granted, the time is reopened for 14 days from the order."}},{"t":"Designation of the record; cost deposit and certificate","d":"7 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nReporter estimate fallback: $300 per day of proceedings.","c":"M.R.A.P. 10(b)(1), 11(b)(1)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Reporter estimate fallback: $300 per day of proceedings."}},{"t":"Appellee’s additional designation","d":"14 days from service of the appellant’s designation.","c":"M.R.A.P. 10(b)(4)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s designation)","note":""}},{"t":"Court reporter files transcript","d":"60 days from service of the designation.\nOne 30-day extension from the trial court; later extensions from the Supreme Court Clerk.","c":"M.R.A.P. 11(c)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service of the designation)","note":"One 30-day extension from the trial court; later extensions from the Supreme Court Clerk."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"40 days from filing of the record.\n50-page limit (M.R.A.P. 28(h)). Record excerpts filed at the same time (M.R.A.P. 30).","c":"M.R.A.P. 31(b)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":"50-page limit (M.R.A.P. 28(h)). Record excerpts filed at the same time (M.R.A.P. 30)."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n50-page limit.","c":"M.R.A.P. 31(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"50-page limit."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\n25-page limit.","c":"M.R.A.P. 31(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"25-page limit."}},{"t":"Cure of a deficiency notice","d":"14 days from clerk’s notice of deficiency.\nOtherwise the clerk dismisses the appeal.","c":"M.R.A.P. 2(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice of deficiency)","note":"Otherwise the clerk dismisses the appeal."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"7 days from service of the motion.","c":"M.R.A.P. 27(a)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for interlocutory appeal","d":"21 days from entry of the trial-court order.\nFiled in the Supreme Court. Not extendable (M.R.A.P. 26(b)). Answer within 14 days after service; 15-page limit.","c":"M.R.A.P. 5(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the trial-court order)","note":"Filed in the Supreme Court. Not extendable (M.R.A.P. 26(b)). Answer within 14 days after service; 15-page limit."}},{"t":"Docket fee after interlocutory appeal is granted","d":"14 days from order granting permission to appeal.","c":"M.R.A.P. 5(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (order granting permission to appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for rehearing","d":"14 days from decision on the merits (Supreme Court or Court of Appeals).\n25-page limit. Response within 7 days.","c":"M.R.A.P. 40(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (decision on the merits)","note":"25-page limit. Response within 7 days."}},{"t":"Petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court","d":"14 days from court of Appeals’ judgment on the motion for rehearing.\n10-page limit. Rehearing in the Court of Appeals is a prerequisite. Response within 7 days. Deemed denied if not acted on within 90 days after the response deadline.","c":"M.R.A.P. 17(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals’ judgment on the motion for rehearing)","note":"10-page limit. Rehearing in the Court of Appeals is a prerequisite. Response within 7 days. Deemed denied if not acted on within 90 days after the response deadline."}},{"t":"Supplemental brief after certiorari is granted","d":"10 days from notice of grant.\n10-page limit; no extensions.","c":"M.R.A.P. 17(h)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notice of grant)","note":"10-page limit; no extensions."}},{"t":"Supreme Court mandate","d":"21 days from entry of judgment.\n7 days after an order denying rehearing.","c":"M.R.A.P. 41(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"7 days after an order denying rehearing."}},{"t":"Review of a denied trial-judge recusal motion","d":"14 days from the ruling, or the end of the judge’s 30 days to rule.","c":"M.R.A.P. 48B","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (the ruling, or the end of the judge’s 30 days to rule)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellate recusal motion","d":"30 days from clerk’s notice of assignment to an appellate court.\nReconsideration by the full court within 14 days of denial.","c":"M.R.A.P. 48C(a)(ii)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice of assignment to an appellate court)","note":"Reconsideration by the full court within 14 days of denial."}},{"t":"Appeal from justice or municipal court, or agency, to circuit court","d":"30 days from entry of the order or judgment.\nNotice and costs filed together with the circuit clerk. Record due 30 days after the notice in on-the-record appeals (UCRCCC 5.05).","c":"UCRCCC 5.04","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order or judgment)","note":"Notice and costs filed together with the circuit clerk. Record due 30 days after the notice in on-the-record appeals (UCRCCC 5.05)."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://courts.ms.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Mississippi Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"Govern procedure in the circuit, chancery, and county courts in all civil suits at law or in equity, subject to the limited-applicability proceedings listed in Rule 81 (which still follow these rules wherever no statute provides otherwise). Construed, administered, and employed by the court and the parties (parties added eff. July 1, 2020) to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.” The note says this unifies procedure only; the traditional separation of law (circuit) and equity (chancery) jurisdiction is unchanged.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint, with a costs deposit in the amount required by law and court rules. A party may proceed in forma pauperis under Miss. Code §§ 11-53-17 and 11-53-19, and the court (on motion of a party or the clerk, or on its own) may examine the affiant about the claimed poverty. Amended eff. Sept. 10, 2025, deleting former 3(b) (security for costs) and 3(d) (accounting for costs) and renumbering IFP as 3(b).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"The clerk issues a summons on filing; service may be by any nonparty at least 18 (process-server fees taxable only up to the sheriff’s statutory fee), by the sheriff on written request (return within 30 days of receipt), by first-class mail with two notice-and-acknowledgment forms (Form 1B; if not returned within 20 days, use another method and the defendant may be charged the costs), by publication (chancery or where a statute allows; once a week for 3 successive weeks, defendant has 30 days from first publication), or by certified mail “restricted delivery” on persons outside the state (complete on delivery or a “Refused” envelope). Individuals are served personally or, if that cannot be done with reasonable diligence, at the usual place of abode with a spouse or family member above 16 plus a follow-up first-class mailing, complete on the 10th day after mailing; infants 12 or older must be served along with a parent or guardian, and the Attorney General is served for the State. A sworn written waiver/entry of appearance must be executed after the day the action was commenced; the action is dismissed without prejudice as to any defendant not served within 120 days of filing absent good cause; and new Rule 4(i) (moved from former Rule 81(d), eff. July 1, 2026) makes listed family, estate, and similar matters triable 30 days after service (adoption, paternity, custody, child support, heirship, partition, will contests, etc.) or 7 days after service (temporary relief, modification or enforcement of custody/support/alimony, contempt, etc.) — 30 days after first publication if served by publication — with a Form 1D/1DD summons returnable to a set date, no answer required, and no default or taking as confessed; unlike a federal Rule 4(d) waiver, a returned Form 1B acknowledgment is actual service (note).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every pleading after the complaint, discovery paper, written motion (other than ex parte), notice, and similar paper must be served on each party, through counsel if represented; parties in default for failure to appear need not be served except with new or additional claims, which require Rule 4 service. Service is by delivery, electronic means (complete when the recipient’s equipment acknowledges receipt, or when the sender obtains an acknowledgment), or mail (complete on mailing), or through the Mississippi Electronic Court System (MEC) where adopted by local rule. Papers are filed before service or within a reasonable time after, but discovery papers need not be filed until used in a pretrial proceeding, and (per the Aug. 11, 2025 amendment) discovery used at trial need not be filed beforehand.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Privacy protection for filings made with the court","s":"Since July 1, 2016, every court and circuit or chancery clerk’s office that stores or files documents electronically (as defined in Miss. Code § 9-1-51) and makes them available online must follow the privacy provisions in Sections 5 and 9 of the Administrative Procedures for Mississippi Electronic Courts.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, statutory legal holiday, or any day the courthouse or clerk’s office is in fact closed; for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded, and a holiday falling on Sunday moves to Monday. The court may enlarge time for cause (before expiration with or without motion; after expiration only on motion for excusable neglect) but may not extend the times under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), 59(d), 59(e), 60(b), or 60(c). Written motions and notices of hearing must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, opposing affidavits at least 1 day before, and 3 days are added after service by mail (not for responses to a Rule 4 summons).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Allowed pleadings are a complaint and answer, an answer to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, a third-party complaint, and a third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought; all papers, including depositions, must be on 8½ by 11 inch paper (depositions also follow the Guidelines for Court Reporters in Supreme Court Rule 11). Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency are abolished.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; denials must fairly meet the averments, and averments other than damages are admitted if not denied. Rule 8(c) lists affirmative defenses (accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, bankruptcy discharge, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, injury by fellow servant, laches, license, payment, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, waiver); the note says the list is not exhaustive (e.g., the right to arbitrate, Tort Claims Act immunity and 90-day notice, the medical-malpractice certificate of consultation, apportionment of fault) and that failing to pursue such a defense while actively litigating ordinarily waives it. Pleadings do not go to the jury room unless admitted in evidence, and every pleading by or for a person under legal disability must disclose the disability unless already disclosed.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity must be stated in the initial pleading; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (malice, intent, and knowledge generally); denial of conditions precedent must be specific; and items of special damage must be specifically stated (the note includes consequential damages, lost profits, and punitive damages). A party ignorant of an opponent’s name may sue under a fictitious name and later substitute the true name (Rule 9(h)), and unknown persons interested in the subject matter may be designated as unknown parties in interest.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and Rule 7(a) designation; the complaint names all parties and its first paragraph gives the names and, if known, addresses of all parties. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, with separate counts or defenses where that aids clarity, and an exhibit to a pleading is part of it for all purposes. Former Rule 10(d) (attachment of documents) was deleted eff. Dec. 26, 2024; the note says noncompliance with 10(b) calls for an order to amend, not dismissal.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings and motions","s":"Pleadings and motions must be signed by at least one attorney of record (or the unrepresented party) with an address; verification is not required unless a rule or statute says so, and the equity rule that a sworn answer must be overcome by two witnesses is abolished. The signature certifies the signer has read the paper, there is good ground for it, and it is not for delay; an out-of-state lawyer’s signature also certifies admission under M.R.A.P. 46(b). Unsigned or sham papers may be stricken, willful violations may bring discipline, and for a frivolous filing or one made for harassment or delay the court may order the party, counsel, or both to pay the opponent’s reasonable expenses and attorneys’ fees (the note also points to the Litigation Accountability Act, Miss. Code § 11-55-5).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"An answer is due 30 days after service of the summons and complaint (or as directed under Rule 4), and answers to cross-claims and counterclaims are due 30 days after service; after a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, the responsive pleading is due 10 days after notice, or 10 days after service of a more definite statement. These times may be extended once only, for no more than 10 days, by a written stipulation of counsel filed in the record; a more-definite-statement order must be obeyed within 10 days, and a motion to strike when no response is permitted is due within 30 days. The seven Rule 12(b) defenses, consolidation, and waiver rules are set out, a granted 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion (without outside matters) carries leave to amend under Rule 15(a), and a court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction must dismiss or transfer to the proper court (preserving circuit–chancery transfers under Miss. Const. §§ 157 and 162, per the note).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Same-transaction counterclaims are compulsory unless the claim was already pending elsewhere, the opposing claim was brought by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the opposing party’s claim is one an insurer is defending (though res judicata or collateral estoppel may still bar it later); cross-claims are permissive, and an omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment under Rule 15 (amended eff. June 23, 2025). In county court, a counterclaim or cross-claim exceeding the court’s jurisdictional limit leads to transfer to circuit or chancery court on motion of all parties filed within 20 days. In a de novo appeal from justice court or another court not subject to these rules, compulsory counterclaims must be added by amendment within 30 days after the appeal is perfected, without the lower court’s amount limit.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may implead a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, but needs leave of court on good cause if the third-party complaint is filed more than 14 days after serving its original answer (amended eff. Feb. 26, 2025; the historical note describes the window as ten days, but the rule text says 14). The note stresses that impleader requires derivative or secondary liability (indemnity, contribution, subrogation, warranty), not an independent claim, and that because fault is generally several only in Mississippi, contribution claims arise mainly for concerted action under Miss. Code § 85-5-7(4). The admiralty subdivision (c) is omitted.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend as of course any time before a responsive pleading is served (or within 30 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar); otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent, and the response to an amended pleading is due within the time left on the original or 10 days, whichever is longer. Unlike the federal rule, the Mississippi rule places no limit on the number of amendments as of course (note); leave to amend is granted when justice requires after a 12(b)(6) or 12(c) dismissal without outside matters, and the note says adding a new defendant also requires a Rule 21 order. Amendments relate back if from the same conduct; a change of party relates back if within the Rule 4(h) service period (120 days) the new party had notice and knew or should have known of the mistake, and a Rule 9(h) fictitious-party substitution relates back (the note adds a reasonable-diligence requirement).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pre-trial procedure","s":"The court may on its own or a party’s motion, and must on the motion of all parties, direct counsel to a conference at least 20 days before the trial setting to consider settlement, issue simplification, amendments, itemized special damages, limits on and exchange of expert reports, exchange of medical records (without abridging the physician-patient privilege), references to a master, Rule 37 sanctions, admissions, and proposed jury instructions or findings. The resulting order controls the case unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"16A","t":"Motions for recusal of judges","s":"Recusal motions must be timely filed with the trial judge and follow the procedures in the Uniform Rules of Circuit and County Court Practice and the Uniform Rules of Chancery Court Practice. Adopted Apr. 4, 2002.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. In subrogation cases the subrogee sues alone if the subrogor has no remaining pecuniary interest, otherwise both sue together. A guardian ad litem must be an attorney and a Mississippi resident who files a consent and oath (and any required bond), with a reasonable fee taxed as costs; an unrepresented infant or incompetent may sue by next friend, and public officers may be named by title.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A claimant may join as many claims, legal or equitable, independent or alternative, as it has against an opposing party; a claim formerly cognizable only after another claim was prosecuted to conclusion may be joined with it, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive rights.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to jurisdiction must be joined if complete relief cannot be had without the person or the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, weighing four listed factors, and the pleader must name unjoined required persons and the reasons.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent embarrassment, delay, or prejudice. The notes (Rule 8 and Rule 20) cite Mississippi Supreme Court decisions requiring a “distinct litigable event” and, in multi-plaintiff cases, pleading of each plaintiff’s claim against specific defendants with time and place, so mass joinder is limited.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed. The Rule 15 note cites authority that adding a new defendant by amendment requires a Rule 21 court order.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A party exposed to double or multiple liability may require adverse claimants to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the stakeholder denies liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim. The stakeholder may deposit the money or property with the court and be discharged, with the action continuing among the claimants.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; the rules contain no class-action procedure (no explanatory note accompanies the omission).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; no rule text or note.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; no rule text or note.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Timely applicants must be allowed to intervene when a statute gives an unconditional right or their interest would be impaired and is not adequately represented, and may be allowed when a statute gives a conditional right or there is a common question of law or fact (also government officers or agencies administering a statute or regulation relied on). The motion must be served under Rule 5 with a pleading setting out the claim or defense; the rule sets no time limit, and the note lists four timeliness factors. A party attacking the constitutionality of a state statute in an injunction or declaratory action must notify the Attorney General in time to intervene.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute (served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4) must be made within 90 days after death is suggested on the record by a served statement, or the action is dismissed without prejudice as to the decedent; the note says interested nonparties such as the estate’s representative must be served for the 90 days to run, and Rule 6(b) extensions apply. The rule also covers disability, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of a public officer’s successor.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Discovery reaches any nonprivileged matter “relevant to the issues raised by the claims or defenses of any party” (the note says this narrowed the former subject-matter scope), including information “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” insurance agreements, and work product only on substantial need; the rule contains no mandatory initial disclosures. Expert discovery is by interrogatory (retained experts: opinions, facts or data considered, exhibits, qualifications with publications from the previous 10 years, cases testified in during the previous 10 years, and compensation; non-retained experts: subject matter and summary), and any designated expert may be deposed only after those answers are received, with the deposing party paying a reasonable fee for deposition time and up to 2 hours of preparation; drafts and most lawyer–expert communications are protected, ESI from not-reasonably-accessible sources is limited, and privilege logs and clawback apply. A party may demand a discovery conference (objections due 10 days after service of the request), protective orders and factors for limiting discovery (burden, cost, amount in controversy, importance of issues) are in 26(d), responses must be supplemented in a timely manner, and every discovery request, response, or objection must be signed with the signer’s address, e-mail, and phone, with mandatory sanctions for improper certification (26(g), eff. Jan. 1, 2020).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party may file a verified petition in circuit or chancery court in the county of residence of any expected adverse party to perpetuate testimony, serving notice like a summons at least 20 days before the hearing (publication or appointed counsel if service fails). The trial court may also allow perpetuation depositions pending appeal or before the appeal time expires; independent actions to perpetuate testimony remain available.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States, before an officer authorized to administer oaths where the examination is held or a court appointee; abroad, on notice before an authorized person, by commission, or by letter rogatory. No deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or counsel, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and to modify discovery procedures, including extensions of time, without court approval unless the extension would interfere with the time set for completing discovery, a motion hearing, or trial (amended eff. Oct. 7, 2021).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Leave is needed only if the plaintiff seeks to depose before 30 days after service of the summons on any defendant (unless the defendant has sought discovery or the special notice for a deponent about to leave the state is used) and for prisoners; notice may provide for telephone testimony and non-stenographic recording, and an organization must designate witnesses under 30(b)(6). A deposition is deemed taken in the county where the deponent is physically present; objections must be concise and non-argumentative, and instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, court-ordered limits, or a 30(d) motion (eff. Oct. 7, 2021). The witness has 30 days after submission to review and sign, the reporter certifies under penalty of perjury, and deposition expenses other than serving subpoenas are not taxable as court costs.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Any party may take a deposition on written questions served with a notice; cross questions are due within 30 days after service of the notice and questions, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect, and the court may enlarge or shorten these times. The officer takes the answers under Rule 30(c), (e), and (f).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial or out of state, unable to attend, unreachable by subpoena, a medical doctor, or exceptional circumstances exist. Objections to admissibility may be made at trial, but errors in notice, officer qualification, form, and transcription are waived unless timely raised; objections to the form of written questions are due within the time for the next round and within 5 days after service of the last questions. Rule 32(c) is abrogated.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Each party may serve up to 30 interrogatories, each a single question (the note counts every distinct question, including subparts), with more only by leave on a showing of necessity. Answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; objections must be stated with specificity (general objections are improper) or are waived, and a party relying on business records must identify them in enough detail to locate them as readily as the responding party (amended eff. Oct. 11, 2021).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things and entry upon land","s":"Requests for documents, ESI, tangible things, or entry on land may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons. Responses are due within 30 days (a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint); objections must state their grounds with specificity and whether responsive materials are being withheld, production must be completed by the time specified, and ESI goes in the requested form or a form ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable. Nonparties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party (or a person in the party’s custody or control) whose physical or mental condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner; the requesting party pays the examiner and advances expenses. Reports are exchanged on request, and requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition. The rule does not apply to Title 93 (domestic relations) actions except in the chancellor’s discretion; adopted eff. Jan. 16, 2003.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after service of the summons; each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons. Lack of knowledge is no excuse without reasonable inquiry, and admitted (including deemed-admitted) matters are conclusively established unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment; the note says such admissions cannot be rebutted by contrary evidence.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"A motion to compel must certify a good-faith attempt to confer (eff. Feb. 20, 2023); evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and expenses including attorney’s fees go to the winner unless the position was substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order may lead to established facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, or contempt; failing to produce a person for a Rule 35 exam, unjustified refusals to admit, and failing to attend one’s own deposition or to answer interrogatories or production requests are also sanctionable (objectionability is no excuse without a protective-order motion). Rule 37(e) adds sanctions for failing to cooperate in a discovery plan or otherwise abusing discovery.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The jury right declared by the Mississippi Constitution or statute is preserved inviolate; the rule contains no jury-demand requirement. Parties waive a jury only by filing a specific written stipulation of waiver asking for trial by the court, and the court may still require a jury trial.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"The court places cases on the trial calendar on its own, on request with notice, or as it deems expedient, after ample opportunity for discovery. The clerk must notify attorneys and unrepresented parties at least 5 days before the trial docket is set, cases must be set at least 20 days before trial unless all parties agree to less or Rule 55 applies (the note says the 20-day wait does not apply to default-judgment hearings), and the clerk notifies parties absent from the docket call within 3 days. Matters where a defendant is summoned to appear at a set date, time, and place (the text still cites Rule 81; that procedure is now Rule 4(i)) and cases with a trial date already set are excluded, and parties may waive waiting periods and agree to a trial date.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"On payment of all costs, a plaintiff may dismiss without order by notice before service of an answer or a motion for summary judgment, whichever is first, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; otherwise by court order on terms (a pending counterclaim survives). Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or obey rules or orders, and a nonjury motion at the close of the plaintiff’s case, operate on the merits unless the order says otherwise (except dismissals for jurisdiction, venue, or Rule 19). Under 41(d), in any case with no action of record for the preceding 12 months the clerk mails notice that the case will be dismissed without prejudice unless within 30 days action of record is taken or good cause is shown in writing (notices go out at least 30 days before June 15 and December 15, with cases presented by June 30 and December 31); the note says a letter to the clerk generally is not enough.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions sharing a common question may be consolidated or jointly heard, and the court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy, preserving the jury right under Section 31 of the Mississippi Constitution. Actions in different counties of a single circuit or chancery district may be consolidated for pretrial proceedings (all matters before trial except dispositive motions), and for all purposes if no jury trial is involved, if all presiding judges agree on consolidation and the presiding judge (amended eff. Sept. 25, 2014).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony","s":"Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless these rules or the Mississippi Rules of Evidence provide otherwise (the note says telephone testimony is within the court’s discretion); an affirmation may replace an oath, and motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony, or depositions. The court may appoint and set compensation for an interpreter, taxable as costs, but interpreters required by the Americans with Disabilities Act are paid by the county and not taxed.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of documents","s":"A domestic official record is proved by official publication or a copy attested by its custodian; a record kept outside Mississippi also needs the custodian’s certificate under oath that the custodian has legal custody and the record is kept under state law. Foreign official records need a final certification (excusable for good cause), a certified no-record statement proves absence of a record, and other lawful methods remain available.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; the note explains that Miss. Code § 13-1-149 requires courts to take judicial notice of all foreign law.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending, signed in blank by the clerk or issued by any Mississippi-admitted or pro hac vice attorney of record (eff. Feb. 22, 2023), and a copy must be served on all parties under Rule 5 immediately once filled out, before the subpoena itself is served; foreign (out-of-state) subpoenas are reissued by the clerk of the county where discovery is sought. Service is personal by a sheriff or any nonparty at least 18, with one day’s attendance fee plus mileage tendered to a nonparty (not required for the State), and proof of service must be filed promptly and served on all parties; a Mississippi resident can be required to attend a deposition or production only in the county of residence, employment, or in-person business (a nonresident only in the county of service) unless the court orders another place. A production subpoena must allow at least 10 days to comply and production may not occur until the 10th day after service (noted on its face), written objections may be served within 10 days (or before the compliance date if sooner), after which production requires a court order, and the ESI, privilege-log, clawback, sanctions, and contempt provisions apply.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party need only state the action it wants or objects to and the grounds, and failing to object does not prejudice a party who had no opportunity to do so.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Jurors are examined under oath on qualifications, by the court (with counsel allowed to supplement) or by counsel, and are selected as provided by statute. Each side gets 4 peremptory challenges before a 12-person jury and 2 before a 6-person jury; the court may allow multi-party sides to exercise them separately or jointly and allow additional ones, but each side must have the same number. The judge may seat 1 or 2 alternates, with each party getting 1 additional peremptory usable only against alternates.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries and jury verdicts","s":"Circuit and chancery court juries have 12 members and a verdict of 9 or more is the jury’s verdict; county court juries have 6 members and a verdict of 5 or more controls.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"General verdicts and special verdicts","s":"Juries return general verdicts unless the court uses a special verdict or a general verdict with written interrogatories; an issue omitted from a special verdict is waived unless a jury submission is demanded before the jury retires. Neither special procedure may be used unless the court gives counsel the written questions a reasonable time before closing arguments; the note says a separation of economic and non-economic damages is needed if a defendant will seek the statutory caps on non-economic damages.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Motions for a directed verdict and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict","s":"A directed-verdict motion must state specific grounds and does not waive a jury; a JNOV motion must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment (or within 10 days after the jury is discharged if no verdict), and the court cannot extend that time. Unlike the federal rule, a directed-verdict motion is not a prerequisite to a JNOV motion (note). If JNOV is granted the court rules conditionally on any new-trial motion, and the losing party may move for a new trial within 10 days after entry of the JNOV.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury","s":"Each party may submit 6 substantive instructions (more with the court’s permission), due at the Rule 16 pretrial hearing or, if none, delivered to the court and all counsel at least 24 hours before trial; instructions are numbered with C, P, or D prefixes (Roman numerals for multiple aligned parties). Objections must be made distinctly on the record before the instructions go to the jury, all instructions except procedural ones must be written, and they are read before closing argument, available to counsel during argument, and taken into the jury room.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In nonjury trials the court may, and must on any party’s request or when a rule requires, find facts specially and state conclusions of law (the note says findings generally should be made in complex cases and are required in marital-asset division). A motion to amend or add findings must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment or findings, and the court may act on its own in the same period; sufficiency of the evidence may be raised later without objection below.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters, referees, and commissioners","s":"Masters (including referees, auditors, examiners, commissioners, and special commissioners) must be attorneys, except non-lawyer experts in extraordinary technical matters and special commissioners for judicial sales and partitions; reference is by written consent of the parties or on an exceptional condition. Unless the order says otherwise, the master holds a first meeting within 10 days of the order of reference; the court accepts the master’s findings unless manifestly wrong, objections are due within 10 days after notice of the report’s filing, and stipulated-final findings leave only questions of law. A special commissioner selling property may be required to post bond.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"“Judgment” includes a final decree and any appealable order; with multiple claims or parties, a judgment on fewer than all is final only on an express determination of no just reason for delay and express direction for entry (the note says certification should be granted cautiously). An order summarily dismissing a post-conviction relief motion under Miss. Code § 99-39-11(2) must identify and file the records relied on (54(c)); a default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed the amount demanded, but since Jan. 18, 2024 other judgments may award relief beyond the demand (54(d)). Costs go to the prevailing party as of course (including the State as plaintiff), may be taxed by the clerk on 1 day’s notice, and are reviewable on motion served within 5 days after notice of taxation.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default when a party fails to plead or otherwise defend and that is shown by affidavit; default judgment always requires application to the court, and a party who has appeared (which the note says can include informal written or counsel communications indicating intent to defend) must get written notice at least 3 days before the hearing, except that default judgment may be entered without that notice on the day the case is set for trial. Entry of default is set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b); the note requires an evidentiary hearing for unliquidated damages. No default judgment may be entered against a person under legal disability or in a divorce or annulment without proof, except irreconcilable-differences divorces as provided by statute (55(d)’s cross-reference to the demand limit still cites “Rule 54(c),” now 54(d)).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party at any time; the motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits may be served before the day of the hearing. Judgment is rendered if there is no genuine issue of material fact, with Rule 56(d) orders narrowing facts, Rule 56(f) continuances, and expenses and contempt for bad-faith affidavits. If summary judgment is denied, the court must award the prevailing party its reasonable expenses of attending the hearing and may award attorneys’ fees if the motion lacked reasonable cause (56(h)).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Courts of record may declare rights, status, and legal relations whether or not further relief could be claimed, may decline when a declaration would not end the controversy, and may advance the case for a speedy hearing; another adequate remedy does not bar relief and jury rights are preserved. Covered matters include deeds, wills, contracts, statutes, ordinances, franchises, and fiduciary and estate questions, and where an insurer has denied or may deny coverage, the party with the claim against the insured may itself seek a declaration that the policy covers the claim (57(b)(2)); the note says failing to try insurance issues separately from liability is ordinarily an abuse of discretion.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Every judgment must be set out in a separate document titled “Judgment,” but a judgment fully adjudicating all claims as to all parties and entered under Rule 79(a) is final even if mistitled, absent prejudice. A judgment is effective only when entered under Rule 79(a); the note stresses that the 10-day post-trial motion deadlines and the 30-day M.R.A.P. 4 appeal time run from entry.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"A new trial may be granted on grounds historically recognized at law (jury cases) or for rehearing in equity (nonjury cases); the motion must be filed within 10 days after entry of judgment, and the court may order a new trial on its own within the same 10 days or, after notice and hearing, on a ground not stated in a timely motion. Supporting affidavits are filed with the motion and opposing affidavits are due within 10 days after service (extendable up to 20 days by the court for good cause or by written stipulation), and a motion to alter or amend must be filed within 10 days after entry. The court cannot extend these deadlines (Rule 6(b)), and the note says a “motion for reconsideration” is treated as a Rule 59(e) motion if filed within 10 days and as a Rule 60(b) motion if later.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time until the record is transmitted to the appellate court, then only with its leave. Relief under 60(b) is available for (1) fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct of an adverse party, (2) accident or mistake, (3) newly discovered evidence, (4) a void judgment, (5) satisfaction or prospective inequity, or (6) any other reason, within a reasonable time and not more than 6 months after entry for grounds (1)–(3); the motion does not suspend the judgment, and coram nobis, audita querela, and bills of review are abolished. Under 60(c), an order transferring a case to another court takes effect 10 days after entry, a motion to reconsider it must be filed within that 10-day period with no extensions, proceedings are stayed while it is pending, and it is deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days of filing.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in evidentiary rulings or other acts or omissions is ground for a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusal to act appears inconsistent with substantial justice; the court must disregard errors not affecting substantial rights.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Unless a statute or court order (for good cause) provides otherwise, no execution or enforcement may issue until 30 days after entry of judgment, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending a motion under Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 59(e), or 60(b), may modify injunctions pending appeal, and stays on appeal are as authorized by statute or otherwise; the State and its officers and agencies need post no bond. Subdivision (e) (stays in favor of the United States) is omitted (note); the 1997 historical note’s reference to a ten-day stay does not match the current 30-day text.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"If the trial judge cannot proceed during trial, another judge sitting in or assigned to the court may finish after certifying familiarity with the record, or may grant a new trial; the same applies to post-verdict or post-hearing duties in nonjury cases.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"Pre-judgment remedies for seizure of person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment — attachment, replevin, claim and delivery, sequestration, and equivalents — are available as provided by law, whether ancillary or by independent action. Garnishment was removed from the list eff. Sept. 1, 1987.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the merits trial may be consolidated with the hearing. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and counsel’s written certification of notice efforts, and expires within the time fixed, not to exceed 10 days (the 10-day limit does not apply in domestic relations cases), unless extended for good cause for a like period or by consent; the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required except from the State and its officers or agencies, and may be excused in domestic relations cases; orders must be specific and bind parties and those in active concert who receive actual notice.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security: proceedings against sureties","s":"A surety on any bond or undertaking given under these rules submits to the court’s jurisdiction and irrevocably appoints the clerk as agent for service, and its liability may be enforced by motion without an independent action, with the clerk mailing copies to sureties whose addresses are known. (This text appears in the full official file but is missing from the notes-stripped working copy.)","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order; otherwise actions seeking a receiver or by or against one are governed by these rules.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"By leave of court and on notice to all parties, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute with the court; the judge may order money deposited at interest in a federally insured bank or savings and loan authorized to receive public funds, payable only on the clerk’s check with a certified order.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A party defending a claim may serve an offer of judgment more than 15 days before trial, and the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing; an unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer; after liability is determined, an offer may be made a reasonable time, not less than 10 days, before the damages hearing.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced, and supplementary proceedings conducted, as provided by statute. To aid satisfaction of a judgment of more than $100, the judgment creditor may examine the debtor or any other person, and their books and papers, in open court as provided by statute or through the Rule 26–37 discovery devices.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a judgment directing a conveyance or other specific act within the time specified, the court may have it done by an appointee at the party’s cost, may divest and vest title to property within Mississippi by judgment, may have the sheriff deliver possession on a certified copy of the order, and may hold the party in contempt.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party (except a creditor of a party to a divorce proceeding), and a nonparty against whom obedience may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process; the note says personal jurisdiction over the nonparty is still required.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"71A","t":"Eminent domain","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; Rule 81(a)(7) lists eminent domain among proceedings governed by statutory procedure (the note cites Miss. Code § 11-27-1 et seq.).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"72-76","t":"[Appeals — Chapter IX]","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi (Rules 72 to 76); the Advisory Committee Notes refer to the Mississippi Rules of Appellate Procedure for appeals (e.g., M.R.A.P. 4: notice of appeal within 30 days after entry of judgment, tolled by timely post-trial motions).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Courts and clerks","s":"Courts are deemed always open for filing, process, and interlocutory motions and orders; trials on the merits are in open court, while other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere in the state, though no hearing may be held outside the district without the consent of all affected parties. The clerk’s office is open business hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, and the clerk must immediately serve notice of entry of every order or judgment on parties not in default (any party may also serve notice); lack of notice does not affect appeal time except as the appellate rules allow (the note cites M.R.A.P. 4(h), reopening if no notice within 21 days).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion practice","s":"Each court must set procedures for prompt hearing of motions, the judge may make orders for advancement and conduct of actions on reasonable notice, and courts may by rule or order decide motions without oral hearing on brief written statements.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk and entries therein","s":"The clerk keeps a general docket noting every filing, process, return, appearance, order, verdict, and judgment by file number, with entries showing the actual date of entry (which starts post-judgment and appeal clocks); a Minute Book with a copy of every judgment and order; indexes and trial calendars; and other required records, which may be photocopied. A case file may not leave the clerk’s office without the court’s or clerk’s permission.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographic report or transcript as evidence","s":"[Omitted] in Mississippi; the note points to statutes on court reporters (Miss. Code § 9-13-1 et seq.) and M.R.A.P. Appendix III.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of rules","s":"The rules apply to all civil proceedings but only to a limited extent (statutory procedures control where in conflict, these rules fill gaps) in 12 categories: habeas corpus; attorney discipline; Youth Court and Family Court Law proceedings; election contests; bond validations; commitment of addicts and persons needing mental treatment; eminent domain; Title 91 (trusts and estates) and Title 93 (domestic relations) of the Mississippi Code; drainage and water-management districts; municipal boundaries; and listed statutory proceedings on bonds, perishable seizures, and receivers. Ex parte matters stay summary, Rule 4 publication satisfies statutory publication requirements, old writs (mandamus, quo warranto, prohibition, etc.) are pursued by motion or action, and old terminology is translated (e.g., demurrer means a Rule 12(f) motion to strike, decree pro confesso means entry of default). Effective July 1, 2026, the special summons and trial-setting procedures formerly in Rule 81(d) moved to Rule 4(i); the Advisory Committee Note still describes them as Rule 81(d).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue","s":"The rules neither extend nor limit jurisdiction, and venue is as provided by statute except that properly joined claims or parties may be brought in any county where any one claim could be, with Rules 13, 14, 22, and 24 joinders treated as ancillary; the note flags a conflict with Miss. Code § 11-11-3(2), which requires each joined plaintiff to establish venue independently. An action filed in the wrong county is not dismissed but transferred on timely motion, at the plaintiff’s expense and to the plaintiff’s choice of proper court, and since Feb. 20, 2004 the court may transfer for forum non conveniens among proper Mississippi venues (the statute separately allows dismissal in favor of another state).","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Local court rules","s":"The conference of circuit, chancery, and county judges may adopt uniform rules, and any court by majority of its judges (the senior judge has an extra vote if there is no majority) may adopt local rules, consistent with these rules. All local and uniform rules must be filed with and approved by the Supreme Court of Mississippi (with an electronic copy) before taking effect and are published in the Southern Reporter.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The forms in the Appendix of Forms are sufficient under the rules and illustrate the simplicity and brevity of statement the rules contemplate.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"The rules are cited as the Mississippi Rules of Civil Procedure, “M.R.C.P.”; e.g., M.R.C.P. 85.","u":"https://courts.ms.gov/research/rules/msrulesofcourt/2026-07-01%20Rules%20of%20Civil%20Procedure%20with%20Advisory%20Committee%20Notes%20(with%20Forms%206-8%20attached).pdf"}]});
